        "eyebrow": "Our story",
        "heading": "It started with a variety nobody sold",
        "body": "<p>Morghew grew potatoes for supermarkets for years — chasing yield, uniformity, and contracts that got tighter every season. The margins kept shrinking and the varieties kept narrowing to whatever stored longest and looked best in a bag.</p><p>So we started growing a handful of heritage varieties for the family table instead — potatoes with real flavour that had quietly disappeared from the shops because they came out came out too knobbly, too varied or didn't yield enough for a packer.</p><p>Chefs started asking. Then their friends. Before long we were lifting Pink Fir Apple and La Ratte for kitchens across Kent and London. Morghew, and The Potato Shop, grew out of that, into what it is today.</p>",
